findings.description:
The code reads attacker-controlled HTTP headers — `$_SERVER['HTTP_USER_AGENT']` (line 35) and `$_SERVER['HTTP_ACCEPT']` (line 36) — and interpolates them directly into a SQL INSERT statement on line 42 without any escaping or parameterization:

```php
$query="insert httpinfo(userid,ipaddress,useragent,httpaccept,remoteport) values('$is_login_id','$remoteipadd','$useragent','$httpaccept','$remoteport')";
$result=execute($link, $query);
```

The original developer-commented-out escaping on lines 28-31 confirms this was intentional (for training purposes). The `$useragent` and `$httpaccept` variables flow from attacker-controlled request headers straight into the SQL string with zero sanitization. Because this is an INSERT statement, the attacker can extract data via error-based or time-based blind SQL injection, or append additional queries if the underlying driver/PHP version allows stacked queries.

findings.recommendation:
Use parameterized/prepared statements instead of string interpolation:
```php
$stmt = $link->prepare("INSERT INTO httpinfo(userid,ipaddress,useragent,httpaccept,remoteport) VALUES (?,?,?,?,?)");
$stmt->bind_param("sssss", $is_login_id, $remoteipadd, $useragent, $httpaccept, $remoteport);
$stmt->execute();
```
Alternatively, restore the commented-out `escape()` calls on lines 28-31, though prepared statements are strongly preferred.

HTML-encode all user-controllable values before output:
```php
$useragent = htmlspecialchars($_SERVER['HTTP_USER_AGENT'], ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');
$httpaccept = htmlspecialchars($_SERVER['HTTP_ACCEPT'], ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8');
```
Apply encoding at the output point, and ideally also sanitize before database insertion.

Use parameterized queries (prepared statements) instead of string interpolation. Replace the query construction with:
```php
$stmt = mysqli_prepare($link, "select * from member where username=? and pw=md5(?)");
mysqli_stmt_bind_param($stmt, "ss", $_GET['username'], $_GET['password']);
mysqli_stmt_execute($stmt);
$result = mysqli_stmt_get_result($stmt);
```
This eliminates the SQL injection class entirely regardless of input content or character encoding.

Use prepared statements with parameterized queries. Replace the string-interpolated query with:

```php
$stmt = mysqli_prepare($link, "INSERT INTO member(username,pw,sex,phonenum,email,address) VALUES(?,md5(?),?,?,?,?)");
mysqli_stmt_bind_param($stmt, "ssssss", $_POST['username'], $_POST['password'], $_POST['sex'], $_POST['phonenum'], $_POST['email'], $_POST['add']);
mysqli_stmt_execute($stmt);
```

Alternatively, re-enable the `escape()` call, though parameterized queries are strongly preferred over manual escaping.

Sanitize the filename before using it in a path: (1) use `basename($_GET['filename'])` to strip directory components, (2) resolve with `realpath()` and verify the result starts with the intended download directory, e.g.:

```php
$safe_name = basename($_GET['filename']);
$file_path = realpath("download/" . $safe_name);
if ($file_path === false || strpos($file_path, realpath("download/")) !== 0) {
    // reject
}
```

This ensures only files within the `download/` directory can be accessed.

Use `textContent` instead of `innerHTML` to set the link text, and set the `href` via the DOM API rather than string concatenation. For example:

```javascript
function domxss(){
    var str = document.getElementById("text").value;
    var a = document.createElement("a");
    a.href = str;                   // browser will encode as needed
    a.textContent = "what do you see?";
    var dom = document.getElementById("dom");
    dom.innerHTML = "";
    dom.appendChild(a);
}
```

Alternatively, if `innerHTML` must be used, HTML-encode and attribute-encode `str` before concatenation using a library such as DOMPurify or a custom encoder.
